# Data integrity

## No substitute data

Do not generate fake orders, sellers, or reviews to stand in for Olist. If `data/raw/` is missing, stop and say so. Do not continue ETL or profiling on invented files.

## Paths

| Path | Rule |
|---|---|
| `data/raw/` | Read only. Do not edit, re-encode, or commit the CSVs. |
| `data/external/` | Marketing Funnel CSVs are real inputs. Do not hand-edit them. Holiday and IBGE region tables are Phase 1 and need a license check first. |
| `data/sample/` | Create or refresh only by running `python src/notebooks/00_verify_and_sample.py`. Default sample is 5,000 orders, seed 42. Do not hand-edit sample CSVs. |

## Types

Read every id and zip prefix as a string so leading zeros survive.

```python
df = spark.read.option("header", True).csv(path, schema=None)  # then cast ids explicitly to string
# pandas: pd.read_csv(path, dtype=str)
```

Do not infer zip columns as integers.

## Sharing

Olist and Marketing Funnel are CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. When the sample or a data excerpt is shared (GitHub, Drive, report), cite Olist/Kaggle and keep that license. Do not present the sample as the full dataset.

## Local vs cloud inputs

- Databricks Bronze reads CSVs from a Unity Catalog Volume, not from a hardcoded laptop path. S1 recorded `/Volumes/olist_catalog/bronze/raw/`.
- Phase 3 warehouse ETL (`src/etl/`) reads that Volume only. Do not add `USE_SAMPLE` or read `data/sample/` or `data/raw/` in those notebooks.
- A later local rerun (R46: S6, Phase 6, Phase 7) would read `data/sample/` only. It is not a Phase 3 task.
